A||||1||kamikaze_222||electrical problems...help!||||9||
Z||000000||kamikaze_222||05-26-2001||01:55 AM||||a couple weeks ago, the starter went bad on my fiero and i had it replaced. then about a week later i go to start my car after eating at taco bell, and it doesn't start. (nothing happened when i turned the key) so i was like "crap, this new starter must be defective". so i went down to pep boys where i had the new starter installed and i told them about it. they said that my battery was probably dead, and i should try to jump it. i doubted that it would work, but i tried it anyways and it worked. the car started right up. i drove it down there and i had them run diags on it, and they said that the battery was fine, the alternator was fine, and their starter was fine. they said i probably left a light on or something, which i KNOW i didn't. so then a couple of days later the car did the same thing, and now the battery has been drained so many times that it is completely ruined. i've concluded that there is a large draw on the bettery, even when the car is turned off, but i can't figure out what it is. there was no draw on the battery before they put the new starter in. but i talked to them and they said they don't do that type of testing on the electrical system. i don't have a whole lot of cash right now, so if anyone can think of how i can find out what is causing the battery to drain, i'd really appreciate it. it drains it quick, too. i was parked for like 15 minutes the first time it drained it. its an 85 4 cyl automatic, by the way. thanks,<P>=chris||||reg||9
Z||000001||Seanh||05-26-2001||12:25 PM||||Well, if you have a multi-meter you can use it to measure the draw off your battery. Just unhook the positive terminal and put one lead from the meter to the battery, and the other to the cable you just removed and see how many amps are being drawn from the battery. Another way to do this if you don't have a meter is to buy the long glass type fuses in assorted amps. Start with 1 amp, 2 amp, 5 amp, and so on.. Unhook the battery, and place the 1 amp fuse between the battery and the cable, hold it there for a min and see if it blows the fuse. If it does, then you have a draw on the battery. You should then go to the fuse panel to see if any of those circuts are pulling the battery down. I would be looking into the starter if you are pretty sure that its the problem. If it is, chances are its bad, or you have a short in the wires going to it. ||||reg||1
